    <h1>DSF member of the month - Johannes Maron</h1>

    <span class="meta">
        Posted by <strong>Sarah Abderemane</strong> on Nov. 20, 2026
    </span>
    <div class="blog-entry-body"><p>For November 2026, we welcome Johannes Maron as our DSF member of the month! ⭐</p>
<p><img alt="Bearded man in a bright orange beanie and a navy puffer vest over a beige knit sweater, laughing as he holds a small black pug puppy up against his chest. Red and orange autumn leaves are blurred in the background." loading="lazy" src="https://media.djangoproject.com/blog/images/2026/11/joe-profile_NFe2D1U.jpg"></p>
<p>Johannes has long been part of the Django community. He is a frequent contributor including project such as django-hijack which has more than 1.7K stars! He is part of the Django Review and Triage team. He is a DSF member since September 2025.</p>
<p>You can learn more about Johannes by visiting <a href="https://codingjoe.dev/">Johannes' website</a> and <a href="https://github.com/codingjoe">his GitHub Profile</a>.</p>
<p>Let’s spend some time getting to know Johannes better!</p>
<h2 id="s-can-you-tell-us-a-little-about-yourself-hobbies-education-etc">Can you tell us a little about yourself? (hobbies, education, etc)</h2>
<p>Johannes Maron, or joe, born Johannes Hoppe in the GDR. Raised in West Germany only to return to his home state for uni. Studied software engineering at the Hasso-Plattner institute, dropped out and lived the start-up life. I still live in Potsdam and have the most amazing pug, son and wife (in alphabetical order).</p>
<h2 id="s-i-have-an-idea-but-where-your-nickname-codingjoe-comes-from">I have an idea but where your nickname "codingjoe" comes from?</h2>
<p>I am joe, and I code. Honestly, I was young, and I am just happy it's not naughtyb0i420 and thus never changed it.</p>
<h2 id="s-how-did-you-start-using-django">How did you start using Django?</h2>
<p>I started using Django for my very first company, I believe it was version 1.3? I have used it for every company I build ever since, and would always do it again.</p>
<h2 id="s-what-other-framework-do-you-know-and-if-there-is-anything-you-would-like-to-have-in-django-if-you-had-magical-powers">What other framework do you know and if there is anything you would like to have in Django if you had magical powers?</h2>
<p>Who said I don't have magic powers? The magic of contributing, we all have it and I made good use of it over the years. But my biggest dream would probably be a browser working group to overcome Django's JavaScript hesitance.</p>
<h2 id="s-what-projects-are-you-working-on-now">What projects are you working on now?</h2>
<p><a href="https://github.com/relays-to/relay">relay</a>, a communication as a service provider. Think Sentry but for email, SMS, or VoIP. Or like AWS, but instead of shooting Katy Perry into space I hope to funnel the proceeds back into the community that it's build upon. Diamond sponsor or die is what I am aiming for. Until I get there, I am offering all services for free to non-profits or community projects.</p>
<h2 id="s-which-django-libraries-are-your-favorite-core-or-3rd-party">Which Django libraries are your favorite (core or 3rd party)?</h2>
<p>I can't live without <a href="https://github.com/django-commons/dj-database-url">dj_database_url</a> and it's siblings like <a href="https://github.com/joke2k/django-environ">django-environ</a>. <a href="https://github.com/heroku/12factor">12-factor</a>, always.</p>
<h2 id="s-what-are-the-top-three-things-in-django-that-you-like">What are the top three things in Django that you like?</h2>
<p>People, people, and people. You know who you are! &lt;3</p>
<h2 id="s-you-have-recently-joined-the-triage-and-review-team-congratulations-i-know-you-are-hosting-now-regular-online-events-on-reviewing-prs-in-discord-could-you-tell-us-a-bit-more-about-this-initiative">You have recently joined the Triage and Review team, congratulations! I know you are hosting now regular online events on reviewing PRs in Discord, could you tell us a bit more about this initiative?</h2>
<p>Like all the best things in life, I just stumbled into it. I wanted to be more active and my PR queue was empty. I went on Discord asked, and people sent me more than I could review that day. I promised review them the following week and the wheel started turning. Fast-forward to some discussion on <a href="https://djangomed.eu/">DjangoOnTheMed</a> which gave me the confidence to make this a recurring thing. A small nudge from Tim (Schilling) to set up a Discord event and thus the FOSS Fridays were born.</p>
<h2 id="s-what-makes-a-good-pr-from-a-reviewers-perspective-whats-the-most-common-mistake-you-see">What makes a good PR from a reviewer’s perspective? What’s the most common mistake you see?</h2>
<p>It's less about the PR itself, but how it came to be. I maintain a lot of community projects where unsolicited feature PRs are all too common. It starts discussion at the wrong end and it requires a lot of mindful communication to convince authors to go back to the drawing board to reject a proposal. Also, "aura farming", patches to large project for the resume, is something I am not too fond of. Mainly because I know that once the PRs is merged, the maintenance burden will fall on me.</p>
<h2 id="s-ai-is-more-and-more-present-in-the-tech-world-especially-in-code-contributions-and-reviews-how-do-you-feel-about-that-as-a-reviewer-of-django">AI is more and more present in the tech world, especially in code contributions and reviews. How do you feel about that as a reviewer of Django?</h2>
<p>I think besides the slop, there is one thing often overlooked. AI has decimated the entry level job market, so you have thousands of graduates turning to open-source to grind for XP. While I can genuinely emphasize, I think we need to steer that raw energy towards something better than generating even more code. Hence, FOSS Fridays. Gain XP and create value through review, not code. Given that AI does most of the corporate code writing these days, I believe that the best skill to acquire.</p>
<h2 id="s-i-know-you-are-maintaining-a-lot-of-projects-related-to-django-thank-you-for-your-work-what-made-you-create-some-projects-in-the-first-place-like-django-health-check-or-django-hijack-for-example">I know you are maintaining a lot of projects related to Django, thank you for your work. What made you create some projects in the first place, like <a href="https://github.com/codingjoe/django-health-check">django-health-check</a> or <a href="https://github.com/django-hijack/django-hijack">django-hijack</a>, for example?</h2>
<p>The funny thing is, I created none of the once you mentioned. I started as a contributor and apparently did a good job at it. So the projects get transferred to me. My process is almost always the same, I build a company, need feature and if I find a package that is unmaintained, I offer my assistance. Only if the technology doesn't exist, I write my own package.</p>
<h2 id="s-how-do-you-manage-to-maintain-so-many-projects-at-the-same-time">How do you manage to maintain so many projects at the same time?</h2>
<p>My solution is twofold. If a project was taken over, I rewrite it from scratch. I am always aiming for an architecture, that can be easily extended via inheritance and avoid adding any settings. Once that is done, I resolve almost all issues with suggestions on how to implement those features within the users own project while constantly trying to improve the documentation. Most of my projects have zero open issues and PRs. The art of a "productive no" is something I could probably fill an entire bookshelf with.</p>
<h2 id="s-you-currently-sponsor-a-lot-of-projects-and-people-via-github-django-python-homebrew-and-others-whats-your-view-on-funding-open-source-and-what-would-you-tell-companies-that-depend-on-it">You currently sponsor a lot of projects and people via GitHub (Django, Python, Homebrew and others). What’s your view on funding open source, and what would you tell companies that depend on it?</h2>
<p>I think you need to separate two things. I use personal donations to show appreciation. They won't pay the bills, but the show maintainers that I care. You just need to decide if you need another streaming to pay for another billionaire's mega-yacht or if those 20 bucks are better spend on your first sponsorships. </p>
<p>Now, corporate sponsorships are a different beast. I believe they are fundamentally broken. For-profits are there to generate profit for their owners. But, they need employees to generate those profits even with all the AI in the world. The one thing you should always ask during an interview, is: "Does the company sponsor any open source projects?" Preferably late in the interview process. It costs nothing to ask, but the pressure will compound. ... Or you do it like my start your own company and do it differently 🤷</p>
<h2 id="s-how-do-you-envision-the-future-of-django-from-your-perspective">How do you envision the future of Django from your perspective?</h2>
<p>I can't wait for Django version 2100. While I might not be around for that, I sure hope it'll happen. As for the DSF, I'd hope that it would eventually be able to build or acquire assets and accumulate wealth to be sponsorship independent. I know it sounds far-fetched, but it doesn't mean we shouldn't strive for a Django Software Fund.</p>
<h2 id="s-after-so-many-times-in-the-django-community-what-makes-you-stay">After so many times in the Django community, what makes you stay?</h2>
<p>I saved the stranges story for last. A long, long time ago, I was kindly offered a refund for my DjangoCon ticket, because of a tweet violating the CoC. Luckily I somehow managed dial down my ego for a minute and apologized like a grown up. The whole encounter changed how I perceived the subsequent conference and our industry as a whole and my role in it. Sometimes we learn more from our mistakes more than from our successes. The Django community has taught and challenged me so much over the years. All those experiences are now inseparable of who I am today. And even if I left, I hope to think I would carry those things with me always.</p>
<h2 id="s-is-there-anything-else-youd-like-to-say">Is there anything else you’d like to say?</h2>
<p>Thank you &lt;3 and go Django 2100!</p>
<hr>
<p><strong>Thank you for doing the interview, Joe !</strong></p></div>

